Subject: [PATCH] rpc: a client parks state in files on the server

A host that serves many conversations on one slot has to keep the state of the
ones not in it. Fetching it costs the network both ways (2.8 GiB each way for a
153K history of Qwen3.8-27B, 50 s out and 65 s back at gigabit) and the host's
memory. RPC_CMD_PARK lets the client leave it on the server's own storage: a
key is a folder under GGML_RPC_PARK_DIR, and the client appends tensor ranges
(or bytes of its own) to a file there, loads a file back into tensor ranges,
lists keys and files, deletes them and asks for the room left (ggml-rpc.h,
ggml-rpc/park.h).

- A file is written once, in order, under a temporary name and renamed when
  whole: a reader sees it completely or not at all. The commit answers with its
  size and a checksum, and a read with the checksum of what was read.
- Every byte moves through one 8 MiB buffer, in either direction and for any
  size of state: a PS5 worker has about 70 MiB to spare.
- The folder is kept under GGML_RPC_PARK_GB (default 128) and the disk above
  GGML_RPC_PARK_RESERVE_GB free (default 16, where the system says how much is
  free): before a write the keys used longest ago are dropped, never one with
  a file open and never the one restored last. The server keeps the order of
  use itself, in the file .used of the folder, as a save or a restore happens:
  a PS5's file times move when a key is saved and not when it is read, so by
  them a small save dropped a 100K-token state that had been restored minutes
  before.
- Files are read with open() and read(): the console's libc reads a stream
  without a buffer a byte per call.
- A title that defines ps5lm_rpc_park_write (PS5LM does) writes the files with
  it: a PS5 title's own writes slow to about 1.2 MB/s after 2 GB.
- A server with a park folder sets bit 7 of the flags byte of its HELLO answer
  (bit 6 is GRAPH_FREE; llama.cpp's own flags count up from bit 0, since
  b12600). A client sends the command only to such a server, as
  an older one drops a client whose command it does not know; an older client
  never sends it. A park command that fails answers with a status and does not
  drop the client.
- GGML_RPC_PARK_DIE_AT=ext:bytes ends the server in the middle of a file, for
  the test of that.

diff --git a/ggml/include/ggml-rpc.h b/ggml/include/ggml-rpc.h
index 168ee774e..6f4cd4cec 100644
--- a/ggml/include/ggml-rpc.h
+++ b/ggml/include/ggml-rpc.h
@@ -35,6 +35,79 @@ GGML_BACKEND_API void ggml_backend_rpc_start_server(
 GGML_BACKEND_API ggml_backend_reg_t ggml_backend_rpc_reg(void);
 GGML_BACKEND_API ggml_backend_reg_t ggml_backend_rpc_add_server(const char * endpoint);
 
+// PS5LM device parking: a client keeps the state of a conversation in files on
+// the worker's own storage (ggml-rpc/park.h) instead of fetching it. A key is a
+// folder; each file in it is written once, in order, and exists only once whole.
+// A worker says in its HELLO answer whether it takes these.
+#define GGML_RPC_PARK_KEY_SIZE  96
+#define GGML_RPC_PARK_FILE_SIZE 32
+#define GGML_RPC_PARK_MAX_DATA  (8u << 20) // the most bytes one command carries either way
+
+enum ggml_rpc_park_op {
+    GGML_RPC_PARK_STAT = 1,     // -> v: free bytes on the disk, bytes parked, the cap, keys
+    GGML_RPC_PARK_LIST,         // arg: first key; size: room in bytes -> ggml_rpc_park_key_entry[]; v: entries, keys in all
+    GGML_RPC_PARK_LIST_FILES,   // key; arg: first file; size: room -> ggml_rpc_park_file_entry[]; v: entries, files in all
+    GGML_RPC_PARK_DELETE,       // key, file ("" = the whole key) -> v: bytes freed, 1 if it was there
+    GGML_RPC_PARK_WRITE_BEGIN,  // key, file; size: bytes to come (to make room) -> v: keys dropped to make room
+    GGML_RPC_PARK_WRITE_TENSOR, // tensor, offset, size: appends that range of the tensor
+    GGML_RPC_PARK_WRITE_DATA,   // size + that many bytes: appends them
+    GGML_RPC_PARK_WRITE_COMMIT, // the file takes its name -> v: its size, its checksum
+    GGML_RPC_PARK_WRITE_ABORT,
+    GGML_RPC_PARK_READ_BEGIN,   // key, file; offset: where to start -> v: the file's size
+    GGML_RPC_PARK_READ_TENSOR,  // tensor, offset, size: the next size bytes go to that range of the tensor
+    GGML_RPC_PARK_READ_DATA,    // size -> the next size bytes
+    GGML_RPC_PARK_READ_END,     // -> v: bytes read, their checksum
+};
+
+enum ggml_rpc_park_status {
+    GGML_RPC_PARK_OK = 0,
+    GGML_RPC_PARK_BAD,        // a bad name, tensor or order of commands
+    GGML_RPC_PARK_NOT_FOUND,
+    GGML_RPC_PARK_IO,
+    GGML_RPC_PARK_NO_SPACE,   // over the cap or the disk is full, with nothing left to drop
+    GGML_RPC_PARK_NO_WRITER,  // a console without its file writer
+    GGML_RPC_PARK_SHORT,      // the file ends before the bytes asked for
+};
+
+struct ggml_rpc_park_req {
+    uint32_t op;
+    uint32_t flags;
+    char     key[GGML_RPC_PARK_KEY_SIZE];
+    char     file[GGML_RPC_PARK_FILE_SIZE];
+    uint64_t offset;
+    uint64_t size;
+    uint64_t arg;
+};
+
+struct ggml_rpc_park_rsp {
+    int32_t  status;
+    uint32_t pad;
+    uint64_t v[4];
+    char     msg[160];
+};
+
+struct ggml_rpc_park_key_entry {
+    char     key[GGML_RPC_PARK_KEY_SIZE];
+    uint64_t bytes;
+    uint32_t n_files;
+    uint32_t pad;
+    int64_t  mtime;   // its last use, seconds since 1970
+};
+
+struct ggml_rpc_park_file_entry {
+    char     file[GGML_RPC_PARK_FILE_SIZE];
+    uint64_t bytes;
+    int64_t  mtime;
+};
+
+// One command to the worker that holds `tensor`, or with no tensor to `endpoint`.
+// False when that worker has no parking (an older worker, or none set up) or the
+// request cannot be sent as it is; otherwise rsp holds the worker's answer.
+// data_in: the bytes of WRITE_DATA; data_out: room for req->size bytes (READ_DATA, LIST, LIST_FILES).
+GGML_BACKEND_API bool ggml_backend_rpc_park(const char * endpoint, const struct ggml_tensor * tensor,
+                                            const struct ggml_rpc_park_req * req, const void * data_in,
+                                            void * data_out, struct ggml_rpc_park_rsp * rsp);
+
 #ifdef  __cplusplus
 }
 #endif
